WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:10.970
- Welcome all of you
to the MacMillan Center

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:12.880
here at Yale University.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.780
So the MacMillan Center is
Yale's multidisciplinary

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:20.510
multi-school international
convening center

00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:23.050
for all kinds of international studies.

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:24.650
We train people in languages,

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:26.820
the histories, the societies,

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:30.470
and the politics of lots
of different countries

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:31.760
across the world,

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:34.660
and America's relationship with them.

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:37.380
We have seven area studies councils here,

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:38.460
the largest of which,

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.350
and I would say one of
the most active of which

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:42.580
is the East Asian Studies Council,

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:45.920
which is increasingly doing more on Korea.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:48.650
And then we have various initiatives

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:51.680
on a whole slew of different issues,

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:53.040
including an environmental issue,

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.167
very relevant to today's topic,

00:00:55.167 --> 00:00:59.283
headed by Nobel
prize-winner Bill Nordhaus.

00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:02.960
So today we're going to celebrate

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.190
the Coca-Cola World Fund Lecture.

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:06.820
This is a lecture that was established

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:10.193
by the Coca-Cola Foundation
in the early 1990s.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:15.290
It's one of the highlights
of our academic year.

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:20.290
We bring in world leaders,
influential public figures

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:23.550
to discuss the world's
most pressing issues

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:26.130
and to interact with faculty and students

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:29.310
from across the Yale community.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:31.890
Previous lectures include
people like Mary Robinson,

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:33.330
the President Ireland.

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:36.810
Raghuram Rajan, who was the
Chief Economist at the IMF,

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:40.150
and then the head of the
Reserve Bank of India.

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:43.330
Raila Odinga, the Prime Minister of Kenya,

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:45.860
as well as leading public intellectuals,

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:47.690
such as Thomas Friedman.

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:50.100
So to introduce our very
distinguished guests today,

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.820
I'd like to invite up
professor Hwansoo Kim,

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:57.700
who is the chair of the
East Asian Studies Council.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.930
Our largest area studies
cancel here at Yale.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:02.250
Hwansoo.

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:05.250
(audience clapping)

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:09.820
- Thank you, Steve.

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:11.760
It's a great honor

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.170
to again welcome
Secretary-General Ban Ki-Moon

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:15.280
to Yale University.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:17.050
And it also our pleasure

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:19.823
to welcome the Secretary General's wife,

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:26.150
Mrs. Yoo Soon-taek and
his daughter Ban Hyun-hee.

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:28.740
Could you just briefly raise your hand,

00:02:29.877 --> 00:02:31.299
Mrs. Yoo first.

00:02:31.299 --> 00:02:32.830
(audience clapping)

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:34.943
And then daughter Ban Hyun Hee,

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:38.193
and his son Ban Woo-hyun.

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:42.320
(audience clapping)

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:45.550
And also former US ambassador Kim Sung,

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:47.098
which yeah, here we go.

00:02:47.098 --> 00:02:49.960
(audience clapping)

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:51.850
So we are especially grateful

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:53.940
that our guests have taken time

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.180
out of their really busy schedules

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:59.863
to fly all the way from
Korea to be here us today.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:05.840
When I first talked to the
Secretary-General via phone,

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:10.750
I think last November
to extend an invitation,

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:14.190
my motive was primarily
to raise the profile

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:15.593
of Korean studies at Yale.

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:19.730
The Secretary-General
accepted the invitation

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:23.470
without hesitation, but
as I hung up the phone,

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:26.253
I thought, what have I done?

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:30.960
The Korean Studies
Program is far too small

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:34.780
to host a figure of such stature,

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:38.630
and thankfully, I would
rescued by Steven Wilkinson,

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:41.190
the Director of MacMillan Center,

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:44.750
who enthusiastically took over

00:03:45.996 --> 00:03:49.890
the coordination of such
a high-profile event.

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:52.160
So I'd like to thank Steven first

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:55.650
and all those staff at
the MacMillan Center,

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:59.030
as well as those on the
Council on East Asian Studies

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:03.133
who worked very hard to
make today's event possible.

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:07.000
As chair of the Council
in East Asian Studies,

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:11.003
it is my pleasure to
introduce our guest of honor.

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:16.130
Born in Korea, Mr. Ban
received a bachelor's degree

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:17.840
in International Relations

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:21.530
from Seoul National University in 1970.

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:25.160
He earned a master's degree
in Public Administration

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:28.230
from the Joan F. Kennedy
School of Government

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:31.313
at Harvard University in 1985.

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:36.950
As all of us know, Mr. Ban
served two consecutive terms

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:40.490
as the Secretary-General
of the United Nations

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:44.053
from 2007 to 2016.

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:47.500
Throughout his tenure at the UN

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:50.350
Mr. Ban strove to be a breeze builder,

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:52.480
to give voice to the world's poorest,

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:54.810
and most vulnerable people,

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:59.810
and to make the organization
more transparent and effective.

00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:04.440
He successfully pressed for
action to combat climate change,

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:08.940
an effort that culminated in
the adoption and rapid entry

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:12.703
into the landmark Paris Agreement in 2016.

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:17.530
Mr. Ban worked closely with
member states of the UN

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:22.180
to shape the 2030 agenda
for sustainable development

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:24.140
and to establish UN women

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:27.050
which had been advancing
the organization's work

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:30.863
for gender equality and
women's empowerment.

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:34.120
Mr. Ban also launched major efforts

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:37.100
to strengthen UN peace operations,

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:39.290
to protect human rights,

00:05:39.290 --> 00:05:41.890
to improve humanitarian response,

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:44.250
to prevent violent extremism

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:47.853
and to revitalize this disarmament agenda.

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:52.420
At the time of his appointment at the UN,

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:55.600
Mr. Ban was the minister of
foreign affairs and trade

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:57.203
of the Republic of Korea.

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:01.120
His 37 years with the ministry

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:05.290
included postings in New
Delhi, Washington, D.C,

00:06:05.290 --> 00:06:09.860
and Vienna and responsibilities
for a variety of portfolio,

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:12.603
including Foreign Policy
Advisor to the President,

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:16.190
Chief National Security
Advisor to the President,

00:06:16.190 --> 00:06:19.910
Vice Minister, Deputy
Minister for policy planning

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:22.073
and Director-General for American Affairs.

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:25.980
Mr. Ban has also been actively involved

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:29.400
in issues relating to
inner Korean relations

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:32.750
by serving as Chairman of
the Preparatory Commission

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:36.800
for the Comprehensive
Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty Organization

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:40.430
After he finished his two terms

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:42.210
as the UN Secretary-General,

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:44.630
Mr. Ban served as chairman

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:46.470
of Presidential National Council

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:51.470
on Climate and Air
Quality from 2019 to 2021.

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:57.560
In 2018, he was elected as chairman

00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:00.337
of Boao Forum for Asia.

00:07:00.337 --> 00:07:03.220
Mr. Ban, along with former
President of Austria,

00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:07.760
Mr. Heinz Fischer was
inducted as a co-chair

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:10.430
of the Ban Ki-moon Center
for Global Citizens

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:11.843
in Vienna, Austria.

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:17.500
Mr. Ban is the Chair of the
Global Center on Adaptation,

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:21.110
an international organization
hosted by the Netherlands.

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:24.950
He was also elected the
chairman of IOC Ethics Committee

00:07:24.950 --> 00:07:27.073
in September 2017.

00:07:28.086 --> 00:07:30.800
Mr. Ban served as the Honorary Chairman

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:34.280
at the Institute for Global
Engagement &amp; Empowerment

00:07:35.133 --> 00:07:40.133
at Yonsei University in
Korea from 2018 to this year.

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:45.630
In February 2018, he was elected,

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:49.150
and has been serving as the
President of the Assembly

00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:52.913
and Chair of the Council of
Global Green Growth Institute.

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:56.700
Currently, he is Chairman
of Ban Ki-moon Foundation

00:07:56.700 --> 00:07:59.330
For a Better Future and
Distinguished Chair,

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:01.560
Professor and Honorary Chairman

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:03.560
of the Future Strategy Institute

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:05.103
at Seoul National University.

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:09.770
So Mr. Ban's current efforts

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:14.240
reside in addressing the
major global struggles

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:17.100
of climate change, the pandemic,

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:19.370
and sustainable development.

00:08:19.370 --> 00:08:20.420
So what is passed

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:23.650
the most international
perspective conceivable,

00:08:23.650 --> 00:08:27.830
Mr. Ban will be speaking on
these pressing issues today.

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:29.680
So without further ado,

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:33.210
please join me welcoming
our distinguished speaker,

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:36.051
Secretary-General, Ban Ki-Moon.

00:08:36.051 --> 00:08:39.051
(audience clapping)

00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:01.470
- Thank you, Professor Steven Wilkinson,

00:09:01.470 --> 00:09:04.173
Director of MacMillan Center,

00:09:05.180 --> 00:09:06.833
and Professor Kim Hwansoo,

00:09:07.850 --> 00:09:11.323
Chair of the Asian Studies Council.

00:09:12.510 --> 00:09:15.810
Thank you for your very warm introduction.

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:20.740
I'm incredibly honored
to follow the footsteps

00:09:20.740 --> 00:09:22.780
of many luminaries

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:29.890
and deliver this Coca-Cola World Fund

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:31.953
at the Yale University.

00:09:32.977 --> 00:09:37.453
Dear students and faculty
members, ladies and gentlemen.

00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:41.250
Thank you very much again for
your very kind introduction.

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:44.510
In fact, you have preempted
most of the topics

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:46.580
which I was going to say.

00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:50.760
So, I dunno how much I can add to this.

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:54.140
Again, in any way is a great honor for me

00:09:54.140 --> 00:09:56.740
to be with you today,

00:09:56.740 --> 00:10:01.680
and try to discuss and share,
first of all, my vision

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:06.680
and my experience and try to
discuss this matter with you.

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:11.530
I was given about 45 minutes.

00:10:11.530 --> 00:10:13.860
I hope that these 45 minutes

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:16.383
will not make anybody sleep.

00:10:18.530 --> 00:10:22.963
I hope it will not make
you a little bit tired.

00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:30.310
I'd like to speak about
first the COVID 19 pandemic,

00:10:31.190 --> 00:10:36.080
and climate change and carbon neutrality,

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:37.713
and global citizens.

00:10:39.290 --> 00:10:40.900
After my lecture,

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:44.850
I will open up the floor
for some questions,

00:10:44.850 --> 00:10:45.893
which you may have.

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:51.770
I'm sorry that my voice has,

00:10:51.770 --> 00:10:54.800
I've been speaking all
day long since yesterday.

00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:59.550
So this is my last occasion
since during last one week.

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:02.983
So I hope you'll understand my condition.

00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:10.010
Indeed, I have wonderful
memories of my April 16 visit

00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:15.550
to this very distinguished university

00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:19.260
where under my initiative,

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:22.250
United Nations has been convening

00:11:22.250 --> 00:11:26.610
sort of colloquium of
University Presidents

00:11:26.610 --> 00:11:29.450
while dealing with climate change

00:11:29.450 --> 00:11:31.660
and other global agendas.

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:34.330
We just wanted to have wisdom

00:11:34.330 --> 00:11:39.330
and some advices from university
academic institutions.

00:11:40.490 --> 00:11:45.000
So Yale University President Salovey

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:47.980
was one of the six Presidents

00:11:47.980 --> 00:11:52.980
on whom we really depended
upon their guidance and wisdom,

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:59.400
and academic intellectual
knowledge on climate change

00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:04.580
and human rights and
sustainable development

00:12:04.580 --> 00:12:06.380
and women's empowerment

00:12:06.380 --> 00:12:10.610
and all the global agendas
United Nations were handling

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:13.060
and hoping to achieve.

00:12:13.060 --> 00:12:17.330
In there, I met the President
Salovey this morning

00:12:17.330 --> 00:12:20.780
a little bit and expressed
my deepest of thanks again,

00:12:20.780 --> 00:12:23.240
after many years after my retirement.

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:25.470
And again, I thank very much.

00:12:25.470 --> 00:12:29.350
I hope our discussions
today will also help me,

00:12:29.350 --> 00:12:34.123
even though I'm a private
citizen, as was introduced,

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:40.087
I'm holding at least more than a dozen,

00:12:40.087 --> 00:12:42.700
a dozen international positions.

00:12:42.700 --> 00:12:47.700
So you can use my voice
if you have anything.

00:12:48.310 --> 00:12:53.310
I have a lot of avenues and way to deliver

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:58.930
and raise the problems
of our global problems

00:12:58.930 --> 00:13:01.163
to the public and global leaders.

00:13:02.010 --> 00:13:04.480
Now, six years later

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:09.480
as a new senseless war of aggression

00:13:10.930 --> 00:13:15.850
in Ukraine has been killing civilians,

00:13:15.850 --> 00:13:19.853
forcing billions of Ukrainian citizens.

00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:25.130
According to UNESCO
resulting in the damage

00:13:25.130 --> 00:13:30.130
and destruction of at least
53 cultural sites in Ukraine,

00:13:31.110 --> 00:13:34.480
since the Russian invasion began.

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:38.943
I feel my words at Yale University,

00:13:40.700 --> 00:13:45.700
and I have been speaking loud
and clearly in my capacity

00:13:46.210 --> 00:13:49.977
as Vice Chairman of the
Elders Organization,

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:56.300
private organization
founded by Nelson Mandela

00:13:56.300 --> 00:14:00.310
and composed of just 10 global leaders,

00:14:00.310 --> 00:14:03.710
of former presidents and
Nobel Peace laureates.

00:14:03.710 --> 00:14:06.480
And I'm working as the
vice-chairman of that.

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:08.760
And we have strongly condemned

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:12.950
this illegal and unacceptable
aggression of Ukraine.

00:14:12.950 --> 00:14:16.460
So this is a part of the configurations,

00:14:16.460 --> 00:14:21.460
but it is now affecting
all spectrums of our life,

00:14:23.820 --> 00:14:26.210
including peace and security,

00:14:26.210 --> 00:14:29.690
and development and human right issues.

00:14:29.690 --> 00:14:33.080
Dear student and faculty members,

00:14:33.080 --> 00:14:36.240
today, we find ourselves

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:40.240
at the center of variety
of converging crisis

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:42.233
and increased uncertainty.

00:14:43.130 --> 00:14:45.970
The ongoing COVID 19 pandemic

00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:49.040
continues to append our economies,

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:52.360
societies and way of life.

00:14:52.360 --> 00:14:55.880
Great power tensions are surging,

00:14:55.880 --> 00:14:58.760
and ongoing conflict in Ukraine

00:14:58.760 --> 00:15:03.760
represents one of the
most dangerous moments

00:15:04.340 --> 00:15:07.470
for global security in decades

00:15:07.470 --> 00:15:12.470
with a profound humanitarian
and economic repercussions.

00:15:12.530 --> 00:15:17.310
At the same time, our planet is on fire

00:15:17.310 --> 00:15:19.853
literally and figuratively.

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:23.440
With the climate crisis deepening,

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:25.723
as the global temperature arising,

00:15:26.740 --> 00:15:30.223
flood rage and wildfires burn.

00:15:31.345 --> 00:15:34.340
COVID 19 and other global crisis

00:15:34.340 --> 00:15:36.260
such as climate change

00:15:36.260 --> 00:15:41.260
have underlined our
fundamental interconnectedness.

00:15:41.430 --> 00:15:43.793
No country is an island.

00:15:44.810 --> 00:15:49.810
Nobody's free from these global threats.

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:55.840
They have also made it clear
that we need global solutions

00:15:55.840 --> 00:16:00.840
born out of cooperation and
partnership, and sustainability

00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:06.360
to address the global
challenges that we face.

00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:10.970
No country, however
powerful and resourceful

00:16:10.970 --> 00:16:15.970
just like United States can
solve these challenges alone.

00:16:17.560 --> 00:16:22.560
We need to forge a stronger
tripartite partnership.

00:16:24.470 --> 00:16:27.480
That means government,
business communities,

00:16:27.480 --> 00:16:29.100
and civil society,

00:16:29.100 --> 00:16:32.070
maybe you may belong to civil society now,

00:16:32.070 --> 00:16:36.280
but some of you will be joining
government and businesses.

00:16:36.280 --> 00:16:40.410
Therefore, these tripartite partnership

00:16:40.410 --> 00:16:43.200
is the most important

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:47.103
to make sure that our
society becomes sustainable.

00:16:51.300 --> 00:16:56.120
I believe that COVID 19 has
also offered us a glimpse

00:16:56.120 --> 00:16:58.730
of the global disruptions

00:16:58.730 --> 00:17:03.613
that will arise from climate
change in the near future.

00:17:04.880 --> 00:17:09.260
These include elevated
public health perils,

00:17:09.260 --> 00:17:14.260
mass displacement,
turmoil to supply chains,

00:17:15.230 --> 00:17:20.230
and increased conflict and
projected economic crisis.

00:17:21.090 --> 00:17:26.090
Both pandemics and climate
change are global issues,

00:17:26.120 --> 00:17:30.880
which will require a strong multi-response

00:17:30.880 --> 00:17:34.420
and international
cooperation and partnership

00:17:34.420 --> 00:17:36.450
as I have stated.

00:17:36.450 --> 00:17:41.450
Under this spectrum of
COVID 19, planetary crisis,

00:17:42.380 --> 00:17:45.830
conflict, and teasing changes,

00:17:45.830 --> 00:17:49.510
we have also made progress in key areas,

00:17:49.510 --> 00:17:53.800
and I know that we have
vital opportunities

00:17:53.800 --> 00:17:56.543
to change the worlds for the better.

00:17:57.580 --> 00:17:59.100
That is why I have established

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:01.670
Ban Ki-Moon Foundation
For a Better Future.

00:18:01.670 --> 00:18:03.710
This is a small foundation.

00:18:03.710 --> 00:18:07.380
This is the only personal foundation

00:18:07.380 --> 00:18:09.400
which I have founded,

00:18:09.400 --> 00:18:14.400
but I'm just working to
make even small contribution

00:18:15.960 --> 00:18:19.473
to make our future better, better for all.

00:18:20.480 --> 00:18:22.690
But to do so, again,

00:18:22.690 --> 00:18:25.570
we must come together in solidarity

00:18:26.602 --> 00:18:31.602
and to beat COVID 19 and
urgency, take climate actions.

00:18:33.330 --> 00:18:36.300
We must redouble our efforts

00:18:36.300 --> 00:18:38.980
to leave up to the guiding ideals

00:18:38.980 --> 00:18:41.170
of the United Nations Charter

00:18:41.170 --> 00:18:45.027
and it's a pledge to, I quote,

00:18:45.027 --> 00:18:48.440
"Save the succeeding generations

00:18:48.440 --> 00:18:50.287
from the scourge of war."

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:53.170
This is the first line

00:18:54.400 --> 00:18:59.400
when our forefathers
established United Nations.

00:18:59.900 --> 00:19:02.510
And this is the first line
of the United Nations,

00:19:02.510 --> 00:19:03.343
Why?

00:19:04.740 --> 00:19:07.910
They were committed after Second World War

00:19:07.910 --> 00:19:10.800
to establish the United Nations.

00:19:10.800 --> 00:19:15.800
We must enhance protection
of civilians during wars,

00:19:16.770 --> 00:19:21.390
as well as the refugees
that those wars make.

00:19:22.750 --> 00:19:26.930
We must strive to better
emphasize prevention

00:19:26.930 --> 00:19:31.930
as a mechanism to address
armed conflict and suffering.

00:19:32.150 --> 00:19:37.150
And we must go further in
bolstering international justice

00:19:38.350 --> 00:19:43.105
and accountability in
the face of war crimes.

00:19:43.105 --> 00:19:45.463
Dear students, ladies, and gentlemen,

00:19:46.570 --> 00:19:51.270
I'd like to begin by providing
you with a bit of an overview

00:19:51.270 --> 00:19:55.400
on the wide-ranging global disruptions

00:19:55.400 --> 00:20:00.400
and impact of this global pandemic.

00:20:01.600 --> 00:20:04.200
Now into is our third year,

00:20:04.200 --> 00:20:09.200
COVID 19 pandemic continues
to alter our personal lives,

00:20:10.560 --> 00:20:14.156
communities, and the world itself.

00:20:14.156 --> 00:20:19.156
With the nearly 515 million global cases

00:20:19.350 --> 00:20:24.350
and at least 6.2 million
tragic global death,

00:20:24.980 --> 00:20:29.120
this pandemic has caused immense suffering

00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:32.693
and serious challenges
all around the world.

00:20:34.070 --> 00:20:37.170
The richest and biggest
country United States

00:20:37.170 --> 00:20:42.170
also had 81.4 million cases
and almost 1 million death.

00:20:45.150 --> 00:20:49.950
COVID 19 has precipitated oversize impact

00:20:49.950 --> 00:20:52.160
on global economic growth

00:20:52.160 --> 00:20:57.160
and pronounced supply
and demand disruptions.

00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:02.510
Such as supply chain
issues are now worsening

00:21:02.510 --> 00:21:05.543
because of the crisis in Ukraine.

00:21:06.480 --> 00:21:11.480
COVID 19 has also amplified
existing inequalities in health,

00:21:12.680 --> 00:21:17.680
labor, housing, food,
gender, and other key areas.

00:21:19.580 --> 00:21:24.580
These impacts, alongside rising inflation

00:21:24.640 --> 00:21:28.780
have converged to create
the most significant

00:21:28.780 --> 00:21:33.633
global social-economic crisis
since The Great Depression.

00:21:34.470 --> 00:21:38.330
For example, the United Nations estimates,

00:21:38.330 --> 00:21:43.330
the COVID 19 cost 225
million full-time jobs

00:21:46.310 --> 00:21:48.343
in the year 2020 alone.

00:21:49.700 --> 00:21:53.650
The Global Network Against Food Crises

00:21:53.650 --> 00:21:57.860
says that the number
of food-insecure people

00:21:57.860 --> 00:22:02.640
is now at 155 million persons,

00:22:02.640 --> 00:22:07.183
a five-year high as a
result of the pandemic.

00:22:08.220 --> 00:22:13.220
And UNICEF warns that one-third
of the world of children

00:22:13.740 --> 00:22:17.570
are unable to access remote learning

00:22:17.570 --> 00:22:20.270
during school closures.

00:22:20.270 --> 00:22:22.830
Under this troubling backdrop

00:22:22.830 --> 00:22:25.860
of the global development landscape,

00:22:25.860 --> 00:22:29.180
it is clear that ongoing recovery

00:22:29.180 --> 00:22:32.193
will be both extended and urge us.

00:22:33.740 --> 00:22:35.960
And this is particularly true

00:22:35.960 --> 00:22:40.100
as vaccine and therapeutic inequity

00:22:40.100 --> 00:22:45.020
continues to extend the
pandemic for so many

00:22:45.020 --> 00:22:47.120
all around the world,

00:22:47.120 --> 00:22:51.650
particularly those in
developing countries.

00:22:51.650 --> 00:22:54.460
To ensure that we recover correctly,

00:22:54.460 --> 00:22:57.900
it is not enough to build back.

00:22:57.900 --> 00:23:02.313
We must build back better
as well as greener.

00:23:03.930 --> 00:23:05.460
Currently, as you may know,

00:23:05.460 --> 00:23:09.400
I'm serving as President of the Assembly

00:23:09.400 --> 00:23:11.560
of International Organization

00:23:11.560 --> 00:23:16.180
called Global Green Growth Institute,

00:23:16.180 --> 00:23:18.550
which is headquartered in Korea

00:23:18.550 --> 00:23:21.943
with the 43 member states.

00:23:23.160 --> 00:23:27.360
Indeed our recovery from COVID 19

00:23:27.360 --> 00:23:32.360
most simultaneously
address climate action.

00:23:32.760 --> 00:23:35.310
Climate action, climate change,

00:23:35.310 --> 00:23:39.840
and COVID 19 are the
two sides of one coin.

00:23:39.840 --> 00:23:42.283
It is tightly corrected.

00:23:44.620 --> 00:23:46.320
We have to understand

00:23:46.320 --> 00:23:48.483
there is a connected interconnectedness

00:23:48.483 --> 00:23:51.933
between all the global challenges.

00:23:52.849 --> 00:23:57.790
Doing so will go a long
way in helping to guide us

00:23:57.790 --> 00:24:02.710
towards a more sustainable
and resilient carbon neutral

00:24:03.840 --> 00:24:07.253
for all people and our planet.

00:24:08.150 --> 00:24:09.510
With this in mind,

00:24:09.510 --> 00:24:13.450
I formally believe that
the international community

00:24:13.450 --> 00:24:15.574
must actively scale-up

00:24:15.574 --> 00:24:19.040
global leadership and cooperation

00:24:19.040 --> 00:24:22.200
in the sphere of public health.

00:24:22.200 --> 00:24:25.940
We must also expand preparedness

00:24:25.940 --> 00:24:30.940
in anticipation of both the
next waves and next pandemics.

00:24:33.210 --> 00:24:37.520
Modern pandemics are
increasingly interrelated

00:24:37.520 --> 00:24:42.120
to biodiversity loss
and closer interaction

00:24:42.120 --> 00:24:44.973
between humans and animals.

00:24:46.610 --> 00:24:50.280
I came to New York and here yesterday

00:24:50.280 --> 00:24:53.243
from my visit to Colombia.

00:24:56.450 --> 00:25:01.450
Colombia is one of the
rich biodiversity country.

00:25:01.700 --> 00:25:06.690
I think they are the
second only to Brazil.

00:25:06.690 --> 00:25:11.283
I flew over this forestry, deep forestry,

00:25:12.260 --> 00:25:15.410
and just one national park,

00:25:15.410 --> 00:25:18.183
one national park should taken.

00:25:19.350 --> 00:25:22.630
I was surprised to find out, first of all,

00:25:22.630 --> 00:25:25.460
the size of this national park.

00:25:25.460 --> 00:25:30.020
I was told that there are 62 such parks

00:25:30.020 --> 00:25:30.920
with the forestry.

00:25:32.440 --> 00:25:37.440
The size of one park
was half of South Korea.

00:25:38.640 --> 00:25:40.003
I was just stunt.

00:25:41.580 --> 00:25:46.060
While I was very much
impressed by the huge forestry,

00:25:46.060 --> 00:25:48.170
but I was also sad

00:25:48.170 --> 00:25:52.983
to have seen so many areas deforested.

00:25:54.090 --> 00:25:55.860
This is a very sad,

00:25:55.860 --> 00:26:00.860
this will really cause
climate temperature rising.

00:26:02.980 --> 00:26:07.200
In any way, the fact of the matter,

00:26:07.200 --> 00:26:12.200
which we are now addressing,

00:26:12.320 --> 00:26:15.740
remains that the global responses

00:26:15.740 --> 00:26:19.303
has been too slow and too weak.

00:26:20.570 --> 00:26:24.253
This is most oppressing task ahead of us.

00:26:25.180 --> 00:26:28.680
I'm afraid to tell you
that young generation,

00:26:28.680 --> 00:26:30.130
students like you,

00:26:30.130 --> 00:26:33.980
you will soon be in charge
to address this issue

00:26:33.980 --> 00:26:38.857
if my generation and
generation of professors

00:26:40.210 --> 00:26:42.430
are not doing enough,

00:26:42.430 --> 00:26:45.573
then I think it'll fall on your shoulders.

00:26:46.640 --> 00:26:50.120
It can set also the stage

00:26:50.120 --> 00:26:54.907
for a more insecure international order

00:26:56.140 --> 00:26:58.840
in the post pandemic era.

00:26:58.840 --> 00:27:02.240
So it has also something
to do with conflict,

00:27:02.240 --> 00:27:04.010
political conflict.

00:27:04.010 --> 00:27:08.780
Thankfully, a variety of
safe and effective vaccines

00:27:08.780 --> 00:27:13.750
and other measures are being
distributed around the world.

00:27:13.750 --> 00:27:16.980
This is incredibly encouraging

00:27:16.980 --> 00:27:21.980
with over 11 billion total
vaccine doses administer to date.

00:27:24.490 --> 00:27:27.800
Coupled with a continuation
of proactive measures,

00:27:27.800 --> 00:27:32.800
such as testing, masking,
ventilations, therapeutics,

00:27:34.320 --> 00:27:39.320
vaccines represent the
global communities best hope

00:27:39.790 --> 00:27:42.540
of ending this pandemic.

00:27:42.540 --> 00:27:44.120
But at the moment,

00:27:44.120 --> 00:27:49.120
only about 66% of the global population

00:27:49.610 --> 00:27:52.900
mostly those in wealthy nations

00:27:52.900 --> 00:27:57.900
have received just one
jab of COVID 19 vaccine.

00:28:00.140 --> 00:28:03.023
I have got four vaccine jabs.

00:28:03.950 --> 00:28:08.230
I think most of you, I was
told that Professor Wilkinson,

00:28:08.230 --> 00:28:09.601
Professor Kim?

00:28:09.601 --> 00:28:11.401
- [Kim] I got three.
- Three, okay.

00:28:12.390 --> 00:28:13.552
I'm better than you.

00:28:13.552 --> 00:28:15.817
(audience laughs)

00:28:15.817 --> 00:28:20.817
But in any way, if you
go to Africa, just a 16%,

00:28:23.120 --> 00:28:27.710
16% all covering African people,

00:28:27.710 --> 00:28:31.300
they have just one jab, one vaccine.

00:28:31.300 --> 00:28:32.543
This is a reality.

00:28:35.010 --> 00:28:39.960
This is both shameful
and counterproductive

00:28:39.960 --> 00:28:44.920
as we strive to sustain
sustainably, persevere

00:28:44.920 --> 00:28:49.920
over this pandemic and return
to semblance of normality.

00:28:51.080 --> 00:28:54.620
Indeed global leaders have a moral

00:28:54.620 --> 00:28:57.500
and humanitarian obligation

00:28:57.500 --> 00:29:00.650
to rectify the current situation

00:29:00.650 --> 00:29:05.650
of a gross vaccine and treatment
inequality and injustice.

00:29:07.800 --> 00:29:12.770
We need to urgently come
together in solidarity

00:29:12.770 --> 00:29:16.730
to vaccinate the world
and end this pandemic.

00:29:16.730 --> 00:29:21.170
And we must remember no one is safe

00:29:21.170 --> 00:29:24.490
until everybody is safe.

00:29:24.490 --> 00:29:25.713
That's a fact.

00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:28.260
If your family member,

00:29:28.260 --> 00:29:32.843
or if one of your friends is infected,

00:29:33.770 --> 00:29:37.867
then you can easily be affected.

00:29:37.867 --> 00:29:42.493
Therefore you must make sure
that everybody should be safe.

00:29:44.570 --> 00:29:45.863
With this in mind,

00:29:46.750 --> 00:29:51.030
all nations should enhance support

00:29:51.030 --> 00:29:55.700
for the UN COVAX mechanism
in its submission

00:29:55.700 --> 00:29:58.833
to get vaccines to those in need.

00:30:00.970 --> 00:30:05.970
It is wonderful that countries
like the United States

00:30:07.040 --> 00:30:12.040
and Europe see some hope as
the virus becomes endemic,

00:30:14.100 --> 00:30:19.100
yet largely treatable with
the vaccines and therapeutics.

00:30:19.570 --> 00:30:21.660
But we must remember

00:30:21.660 --> 00:30:26.660
that this virus remains formidable enemy

00:30:26.760 --> 00:30:29.333
and not to let our guards down,

00:30:30.170 --> 00:30:35.170
especially as much of the
world remains unprotected.

00:30:36.040 --> 00:30:37.610
With this in mind, again,

00:30:37.610 --> 00:30:42.610
I reiterate the UN Secret
General, António Guterres appeal

00:30:43.710 --> 00:30:48.160
for a global vaccination
plan to double production,

00:30:48.160 --> 00:30:53.160
and which at least 70%
of the world's population

00:30:53.370 --> 00:30:58.370
by mid-2022, just two months.

00:30:59.720 --> 00:31:04.140
We have both the resources
and ability to do so,

00:31:04.140 --> 00:31:07.710
but lack the political will.

00:31:07.710 --> 00:31:10.990
I have found during my of tenure service

00:31:10.990 --> 00:31:12.780
at the United Nations,

00:31:12.780 --> 00:31:16.840
it is just because of
lack of political will,

00:31:16.840 --> 00:31:20.100
the political leaders do not have

00:31:20.100 --> 00:31:23.120
such a firm determined will.

00:31:23.120 --> 00:31:27.040
If they show some determination,

00:31:27.040 --> 00:31:29.040
then I think we can handle this matter

00:31:29.040 --> 00:31:31.633
much, much earlier and easily.

00:31:32.870 --> 00:31:35.080
Dear students and professors,

00:31:35.080 --> 00:31:40.080
let me just now go back to
the origin of the problems

00:31:40.740 --> 00:31:43.623
that is climate change phenomena.

00:31:48.270 --> 00:31:50.340
You must have heard many times that

00:31:51.970 --> 00:31:56.150
since the industrialization
about 200 years ago,

00:31:56.150 --> 00:32:00.240
humanity has achieved
remarkable economic growth

00:32:00.240 --> 00:32:03.480
and technological progress.

00:32:03.480 --> 00:32:06.140
However, in the process,

00:32:06.140 --> 00:32:10.420
serious environmental damage occurred

00:32:10.420 --> 00:32:14.580
and global warming and
climate change has ensued

00:32:14.580 --> 00:32:19.580
as a result of reckless
human-made carbon emissions.

00:32:20.920 --> 00:32:24.830
As biodiversity and habitats are destroyed

00:32:24.830 --> 00:32:27.450
due to environmental destruction

00:32:27.450 --> 00:32:32.450
wild animals have increasingly
become into contact

00:32:33.120 --> 00:32:34.350
with the humans.

00:32:34.350 --> 00:32:39.350
And as a result, novel viruses
and pandemic have spread.

00:32:41.280 --> 00:32:46.060
Indeed, nearly all of the viruses

00:32:46.060 --> 00:32:48.940
that have emerged in the 21st century

00:32:50.250 --> 00:32:55.193
from SAR in 2000, to COVID 19 recently,

00:32:56.100 --> 00:32:58.030
share the characteristics

00:32:58.030 --> 00:33:01.800
of being common infectious diseases

00:33:01.800 --> 00:33:05.470
between humans and animals.

00:33:05.470 --> 00:33:09.160
The degradation of our
ecosystem by human beings

00:33:09.160 --> 00:33:11.550
in the name of development.

00:33:11.550 --> 00:33:16.550
I think we have become
very close to animals.

00:33:18.470 --> 00:33:21.633
There's the main reason why
we are suffering from now.

00:33:23.530 --> 00:33:26.030
In 2021 alone,

00:33:26.030 --> 00:33:30.917
historic rain dramatically
deloused Central China

00:33:32.900 --> 00:33:35.550
and in the Mediterranean

00:33:35.550 --> 00:33:39.480
has been in flames with
a terrifying wildfires

00:33:40.770 --> 00:33:44.530
burning in Greece, Turkey, Africa.

00:33:44.530 --> 00:33:47.470
I think Western Coast of United States

00:33:47.470 --> 00:33:51.493
has also suffered a lot
because of these wildfires.

00:33:52.670 --> 00:33:55.200
The catastrophic flooding submerged

00:33:56.290 --> 00:34:01.290
parts of central Europe
like Germany, Belgium,

00:34:02.160 --> 00:34:06.363
underwater last September, last summer.

00:34:07.350 --> 00:34:09.400
I was told that the flood,

00:34:09.400 --> 00:34:13.560
which devastated some part of Germany

00:34:13.560 --> 00:34:18.560
was such first one in
1000 years, 1000 years.

00:34:20.119 --> 00:34:25.119
You may not believe it, but is
science which had determined

00:34:27.590 --> 00:34:32.240
that it was the worst case
in 1000 years in Germany,

00:34:32.240 --> 00:34:34.943
in such a prosper, wealthy countries.

00:34:36.020 --> 00:34:41.020
The five hottest years of
globally recorded temperatures

00:34:41.230 --> 00:34:46.000
have now all occurred since 2015,

00:34:46.000 --> 00:34:48.420
during last seven years.

00:34:48.420 --> 00:34:50.953
July 2021, last year,

00:34:51.940 --> 00:34:55.713
was the earth's hottest
month ever recorded.

00:34:58.370 --> 00:35:02.830
The fifth extension
people saying that may...

00:35:03.730 --> 00:35:08.480
The sixth extension may
occur in hundred years

00:35:08.480 --> 00:35:12.310
if we not take urgent action.

00:35:12.310 --> 00:35:15.680
Then when this fifth
mass extinction happened,

00:35:15.680 --> 00:35:18.743
what does this mass extinction means?

00:35:19.950 --> 00:35:24.950
65 million years ago, most of the animals,

00:35:25.730 --> 00:35:30.730
including dinosaurs just perished.

00:35:30.820 --> 00:35:33.280
65 million years ago.

00:35:33.280 --> 00:35:35.670
Then why we have to suffer

00:35:35.670 --> 00:35:40.670
from this sixth mass extinction
in just a hundred years.

00:35:41.360 --> 00:35:43.210
This is not my warning.

00:35:43.210 --> 00:35:45.990
This is the warning of scientist

00:35:45.990 --> 00:35:48.733
and climatologist and geologist.

00:35:50.730 --> 00:35:55.730
So we have to make sure
that within 100 years,

00:35:56.880 --> 00:35:59.880
I think we may not be here at that time,

00:35:59.880 --> 00:36:04.880
but some of your grandparents
and great grandparent,

00:36:06.500 --> 00:36:09.860
I mean, sons, daughters

00:36:09.860 --> 00:36:13.070
will suffer from this mass extinction.

00:36:13.070 --> 00:36:15.410
Geologists say that mass extinction

00:36:15.410 --> 00:36:19.670
means that at least 70% of all species

00:36:19.670 --> 00:36:22.943
will perish when it happens.

00:36:24.150 --> 00:36:27.120
We have to really do much, much more

00:36:27.120 --> 00:36:32.120
to prevent this kind of a mass
extinction from happening.

00:36:33.230 --> 00:36:35.620
Here in the United
States, you know very well

00:36:36.620 --> 00:36:41.620
the realities of rapidly
accelerating climate crisis

00:36:41.690 --> 00:36:45.800
Summers are getting
both longer and hotter.

00:36:45.800 --> 00:36:50.720
And the frequency and
intensity of flooding, storms,

00:36:50.720 --> 00:36:55.720
and heavy heatwaves is
increasing each and every year.

00:36:56.980 --> 00:37:00.290
Warming is not just a future threat.

00:37:00.290 --> 00:37:03.750
A landmark study released recently

00:37:03.750 --> 00:37:07.190
in the scientific journal, The Nature,

00:37:07.190 --> 00:37:12.190
says that nearly 37% of all
global heat-related death

00:37:15.240 --> 00:37:20.240
can now be blamed on
human-caused climate change.

00:37:20.710 --> 00:37:25.070
How can we mitigate these dire threats?

00:37:25.070 --> 00:37:28.360
Western nations, such
as the United States,

00:37:28.360 --> 00:37:31.210
United Kingdom of European Union

00:37:31.210 --> 00:37:34.750
certainly cannot do it alone,

00:37:34.750 --> 00:37:39.080
nor can Asian countries,
Korea, Japan, China

00:37:39.080 --> 00:37:40.663
can do it alone.

00:37:41.600 --> 00:37:46.600
What is needed is an
unprecedented global effort

00:37:47.710 --> 00:37:52.710
propelled by multilateralism,
solidarity, and partnership.

00:37:54.150 --> 00:37:56.520
At least in our common effort,

00:37:56.520 --> 00:37:59.593
we lost four years, at least four years,

00:38:01.480 --> 00:38:05.410
during the time of Trump administration,

00:38:05.410 --> 00:38:09.530
I'm not here to criticize any nations.

00:38:09.530 --> 00:38:13.140
When I was Secretary-General
at the United Nations,

00:38:13.140 --> 00:38:18.100
I was very careful not
to directly confront

00:38:18.100 --> 00:38:20.380
any member state or head of state.

00:38:20.380 --> 00:38:22.068
Now I'm a private citizen.

00:38:22.068 --> 00:38:24.370
(audience laughing)

00:38:24.370 --> 00:38:27.890
I have been speaking out quite frequently

00:38:29.460 --> 00:38:31.860
that President Trump's decision

00:38:31.860 --> 00:38:35.940
to withdraw from the Paris
Climate Change Agreement

00:38:36.840 --> 00:38:41.193
was first of all, politically,
very shortsighted,

00:38:42.140 --> 00:38:46.170
and economically, very irresponsible.

00:38:46.170 --> 00:38:49.063
Scientifically speaking, dead wrong.

00:38:50.330 --> 00:38:53.180
And I wondered, his name

00:38:53.180 --> 00:38:56.673
will remain in the wrong side of history.

00:38:57.900 --> 00:38:59.197
I have been speaking out.

00:39:01.510 --> 00:39:06.510
How much time, and energy,
and passion, and difficulties,

00:39:06.560 --> 00:39:10.650
and challenges I have
experienced during 10 years

00:39:10.650 --> 00:39:15.650
to make sure that climate
change would be agreement.

00:39:16.560 --> 00:39:19.533
You do not understand how
difficult it has been.

00:39:21.040 --> 00:39:24.960
195 state parties assigned,

00:39:24.960 --> 00:39:29.960
but this is almost unprecedented
way of decision-making.

00:39:30.230 --> 00:39:33.603
Normally you make decision by majority,

00:39:34.720 --> 00:39:39.720
or two-third majority, or you
can go three-quarter majority,

00:39:40.430 --> 00:39:42.540
but normally, two-thirds majority

00:39:42.540 --> 00:39:45.340
is most a difficult process,

00:39:45.340 --> 00:39:50.200
but this is a hundred
percent absolute unanimity.

00:39:50.200 --> 00:39:55.137
Even a single country
could kill the decision.

00:39:56.820 --> 00:40:01.820
That might have happened in reality.

00:40:01.940 --> 00:40:04.853
At the last minute of the gathering,

00:40:06.130 --> 00:40:08.380
I thought that everything was ready.

00:40:08.380 --> 00:40:10.010
Everybody was ready,

00:40:10.010 --> 00:40:11.790
but there was only one country

00:40:13.080 --> 00:40:17.610
who was indicating that
they would not support it.

00:40:17.610 --> 00:40:19.163
There was a big crisis.

00:40:21.110 --> 00:40:26.110
John Kerry, myself, other Americans,

00:40:26.110 --> 00:40:28.490
everybody went to that delegation

00:40:28.490 --> 00:40:31.203
really wanted to, if
we can break the neck.

00:40:33.000 --> 00:40:37.010
But lastly, through
some sort of negotiation

00:40:38.820 --> 00:40:42.760
between myself and the party concerned,

00:40:42.760 --> 00:40:47.287
we were able to finally
adopt this agreement.

00:40:49.670 --> 00:40:53.420
And I made sure that it came into effect

00:40:53.420 --> 00:40:56.630
before I leave the United Nations.

00:40:56.630 --> 00:41:00.450
It was just a matter of
one and a half a month,

00:41:00.450 --> 00:41:02.000
one and a half a month.

00:41:02.000 --> 00:41:06.500
My term ended just one
and a half a month later

00:41:06.500 --> 00:41:10.563
after this Paris Climate
Change became effective.

00:41:11.870 --> 00:41:13.210
It is a law.

00:41:13.210 --> 00:41:14.143
It's a treaty.

00:41:15.810 --> 00:41:17.193
Two months later,

00:41:18.050 --> 00:41:22.510
as soon as when President
Trump came, he just withdrawn.

00:41:22.510 --> 00:41:25.970
But fortunately, just US withdrawal

00:41:25.970 --> 00:41:28.790
didn't affect the existence

00:41:28.790 --> 00:41:32.473
and effectiveness of this agreement.

00:41:33.540 --> 00:41:35.903
Had it not been effective,

00:41:36.940 --> 00:41:40.420
and Trump redo from this,

00:41:40.420 --> 00:41:44.970
then we would not have climate
change agreement even now.

00:41:44.970 --> 00:41:49.970
It might have been very,
very difficult moment.

00:41:50.760 --> 00:41:55.760
And when President Biden just joined

00:41:57.800 --> 00:41:59.730
as he's the first president

00:42:00.700 --> 00:42:04.630
to rejoin the Paris
Climate Change Agreement,

00:42:04.630 --> 00:42:08.460
I really had the sigh of deep relief.

00:42:08.460 --> 00:42:12.520
This is real story I'm telling you

00:42:12.520 --> 00:42:14.590
from my own experience.

00:42:14.590 --> 00:42:16.423
In any way, ladies and gentlemen,

00:42:17.420 --> 00:42:19.630
I still believe that Paris Agreement

00:42:19.630 --> 00:42:22.510
offers us our best hope

00:42:23.610 --> 00:42:28.610
to persevere of the serious
threats to our planet,

00:42:28.610 --> 00:42:33.610
but we must urgently step
up implementing commitment.

00:42:35.620 --> 00:42:37.480
We don't have a planet B

00:42:38.790 --> 00:42:43.710
because don't have a plan B.

00:42:43.710 --> 00:42:46.210
We have only plan A

00:42:46.210 --> 00:42:49.860
because we don't have another planet B.

00:42:49.860 --> 00:42:54.377
This is something which I
have been repeating saying.

00:42:55.970 --> 00:42:58.020
You may quote this one,

00:42:58.020 --> 00:43:03.020
I don't claim any copyright or whatever.

00:43:06.910 --> 00:43:10.163
Ladies and gentlemen,
we have no time to lose.

00:43:12.210 --> 00:43:17.210
This overing IPCC report
released in March this year,

00:43:18.735 --> 00:43:23.443
explicitly concludes by warning that,

00:43:25.027 --> 00:43:30.027
"Any further delay in consorted
anticipated global action

00:43:31.590 --> 00:43:34.660
on adaptation and mitigation

00:43:34.660 --> 00:43:39.660
will miss a brief and rapidly
closing window of opportunity

00:43:40.800 --> 00:43:45.677
to secure a livable and
sustainable future for all."

00:43:46.610 --> 00:43:48.930
This is what the IPCC,

00:43:48.930 --> 00:43:53.930
composed of more than 2000
scientists, climatologists

00:43:55.160 --> 00:43:57.580
has been repeatedly warning.

00:43:57.580 --> 00:44:02.580
So we must listen to the
voices of scientists.

00:44:04.340 --> 00:44:05.926
Doing so will be the difference

00:44:05.926 --> 00:44:10.690
between either charting a secure

00:44:10.690 --> 00:44:15.690
and prosper future for
students, such as yourselves

00:44:17.560 --> 00:44:20.650
and our next generation.

00:44:20.650 --> 00:44:23.367
If we do not do that,

00:44:23.367 --> 00:44:28.367
we will embark on a future
that condemns young people

00:44:29.170 --> 00:44:31.940
and their children to a planet

00:44:31.940 --> 00:44:36.113
besieged by flames, heat, and flood.

00:44:38.080 --> 00:44:41.453
What happens is up to us.

00:44:47.060 --> 00:44:50.263
But I'm again saying that we must act now.

00:44:51.479 --> 00:44:53.300
With this in mind,

00:44:53.300 --> 00:44:57.390
in order to faithfully
implement the Paris Agreement

00:44:57.390 --> 00:44:59.960
and push it even further,

00:44:59.960 --> 00:45:04.020
all stakeholders need
to expand their actions

00:45:04.020 --> 00:45:05.780
to cut emissions

00:45:05.780 --> 00:45:10.140
and expect the rapid decarbonization

00:45:10.140 --> 00:45:14.583
of the global economy to
achieve carbon neutrality.

00:45:16.240 --> 00:45:19.520
Fortunately, many countries,
including the United States,

00:45:19.520 --> 00:45:23.880
European Union, Republic of Korea, Japan,

00:45:23.880 --> 00:45:27.870
I think they have announced
the carbon neutrality 2050.

00:45:27.870 --> 00:45:31.663
China has announced
carbon neutrality 2060.

00:45:33.052 --> 00:45:36.200
India has announced 2070,

00:45:37.450 --> 00:45:41.883
that I am very much disappointed.

00:45:43.700 --> 00:45:46.000
World leaders have understood

00:45:46.000 --> 00:45:51.000
the Chinese commitment by 2060
was accepted and understood,

00:45:57.180 --> 00:46:01.910
But when India said 2070,

00:46:01.910 --> 00:46:03.940
that has really shaken

00:46:03.940 --> 00:46:08.116
and destroyed full commitment

00:46:08.116 --> 00:46:13.116
during COP26 in Glasgow last year.

00:46:16.270 --> 00:46:19.373
Our target was that by the end of 2050,

00:46:26.360 --> 00:46:28.970
this carbon neutrality should be committed

00:46:28.970 --> 00:46:30.940
then because of India...

00:46:33.730 --> 00:46:38.280
Just as I said, one country
can kill all this agreement.

00:46:38.280 --> 00:46:42.240
There was no other way
but to agree by saying

00:46:42.240 --> 00:46:47.240
that around the middle of
the century, not by 2050.

00:46:54.490 --> 00:46:57.093
So it was a little bit watered down,

00:46:58.090 --> 00:47:01.690
and there was no agreement
for financial contribution

00:47:01.690 --> 00:47:03.763
by the rich countries.

00:47:05.550 --> 00:47:09.150
I'm deeply concerned
and deeply disappointed

00:47:09.150 --> 00:47:12.230
that United States and European Union,

00:47:12.230 --> 00:47:14.430
they have not kept their promise,

00:47:14.430 --> 00:47:17.293
which they made publicly in 2009.

00:47:18.190 --> 00:47:23.190
Just how many years?

00:47:23.540 --> 00:47:25.517
13 years has passed.

00:47:25.517 --> 00:47:28.550
They promised that they would provide

00:47:28.550 --> 00:47:33.450
a hundred billion dollars
every year by 2020.

00:47:33.450 --> 00:47:38.280
Then by this time hundred
billion dollars should be ready

00:47:38.280 --> 00:47:41.700
to be distributed to
developing countries now,

00:47:41.700 --> 00:47:45.220
but even just 13 years after

00:47:45.220 --> 00:47:49.050
they are not still keeping their promises.

00:47:49.050 --> 00:47:51.930
This is the lack of political will,

00:47:51.930 --> 00:47:56.070
what I have been always
criticizing publicly

00:47:56.070 --> 00:47:57.570
to the member state.

00:47:57.570 --> 00:48:02.570
And I'm again urging that big
countries, rich countries,

00:48:04.960 --> 00:48:09.960
first, United States, and
European Union, and Japan,

00:48:10.040 --> 00:48:11.240
and including Korea too,

00:48:12.090 --> 00:48:16.660
they have mobilized at least
a hundred billion every year.

00:48:19.720 --> 00:48:21.540
So ladies and gentlemen,

00:48:21.540 --> 00:48:24.400
what I'm just trying to say is that

00:48:24.400 --> 00:48:28.760
we have to do all what we have to do.

00:48:28.760 --> 00:48:31.900
You may feel that you
know, I don't understand,

00:48:31.900 --> 00:48:33.970
or I don't have any responsibility,

00:48:33.970 --> 00:48:36.690
but I think there are some ways

00:48:36.690 --> 00:48:38.683
which you can help at this time.

00:48:39.990 --> 00:48:44.070
You may quote me, you may quote IPCC,

00:48:44.070 --> 00:48:48.230
but you may raise your
voices to your governors,

00:48:48.230 --> 00:48:53.230
to your senators, and
parliamentarians, and your presidents.

00:48:54.840 --> 00:48:56.990
Then tell them that,

00:48:56.990 --> 00:49:00.080
please make sure that this is the world

00:49:00.080 --> 00:49:05.080
where I and my parent and my
sons and daughters will live.

00:49:07.220 --> 00:49:09.543
So just make sure that during your time,

00:49:10.480 --> 00:49:12.600
keep this promise, which you have made

00:49:13.570 --> 00:49:15.853
carbon neutrality by 2050.

00:49:16.740 --> 00:49:21.740
I think you have such a
power and raise your voice.

00:49:24.792 --> 00:49:29.710
You see that my voice is
already very much down

00:49:29.710 --> 00:49:32.900
because I have been always
speaking out like this way

00:49:32.900 --> 00:49:34.330
with a passion.

00:49:34.330 --> 00:49:39.263
By the end of the day, I'm
almost losing my voice.

00:49:40.150 --> 00:49:43.560
I have been speaking all
the times like this way,

00:49:43.560 --> 00:49:45.860
speaking out to the world leaders.

00:49:45.860 --> 00:49:49.970
Now I'm relying on your power.

00:49:49.970 --> 00:49:51.033
You have the vote.

00:49:52.400 --> 00:49:55.860
If they are not committed,
you can change the candidate.

00:49:55.860 --> 00:49:57.990
You can change the President.

00:49:57.990 --> 00:50:01.707
This is your prerogative
as a citizens of the world.

00:50:01.707 --> 00:50:04.260
Again, I'm really asking,

00:50:04.260 --> 00:50:09.260
we simply must adapt and
act now or perish later.

00:50:11.070 --> 00:50:13.303
Do you want to be perished?

00:50:14.920 --> 00:50:17.320
Leaders must elevate their political will

00:50:18.450 --> 00:50:21.910
for climate action, and industries,

00:50:21.910 --> 00:50:26.480
and businesses must act in partnership

00:50:26.480 --> 00:50:29.570
and leading educational institutions,

00:50:29.570 --> 00:50:33.950
such as Yale University and
young people like yourselves

00:50:33.950 --> 00:50:37.957
have a critical role
to play in this regard.

00:50:40.220 --> 00:50:44.703
We need all hands on the tech approach.

00:50:45.660 --> 00:50:49.743
Not a single person is exempt from this.

00:50:52.590 --> 00:50:55.190
You may think that I'm just a student,

00:50:55.190 --> 00:50:57.150
but you have the voting, right?

00:50:57.150 --> 00:50:59.400
You have the voice again,

00:50:59.400 --> 00:51:02.580
and you, the younger generation,

00:51:02.580 --> 00:51:06.220
are the future leaders and global citizens

00:51:06.220 --> 00:51:11.220
that I firmly believe
can lead us to brighter

00:51:11.300 --> 00:51:16.093
and more sustainable and
more inclusive future.

00:51:16.930 --> 00:51:21.930
Ladies and gentlemen, let me
just tell you as I conclude

00:51:22.400 --> 00:51:24.253
about global citizenship.

00:51:26.440 --> 00:51:29.563
I have been meeting so many world leaders.

00:51:30.420 --> 00:51:33.923
They all claim that I'm the leader,

00:51:34.840 --> 00:51:39.600
but I have not seen among them,

00:51:39.600 --> 00:51:44.600
none many truly genuinely global leaders

00:51:45.130 --> 00:51:46.870
with the global citizens.

00:51:46.870 --> 00:51:49.963
At first, they are the national leaders.

00:51:53.559 --> 00:51:56.223
What does then mean
then global citizenship.

00:51:57.120 --> 00:52:02.120
Global citizens means that
those who identify themselves,

00:52:05.300 --> 00:52:08.360
not merely as a member of the community,

00:52:08.360 --> 00:52:10.753
but citizen of the world.

00:52:12.130 --> 00:52:14.320
The passport you may carry,

00:52:14.320 --> 00:52:18.080
your nationality does not
mean much at this time

00:52:19.330 --> 00:52:24.323
with a transformative
development of communication,

00:52:25.260 --> 00:52:30.100
transportation, information,
science, and technology.

00:52:30.100 --> 00:52:34.083
I think we are living
in a very small world.

00:52:35.000 --> 00:52:36.480
Wherever I'm speaking now

00:52:36.480 --> 00:52:41.420
can be delivered to the
corner of the end of the world

00:52:41.420 --> 00:52:43.313
within a fraction of a second.

00:52:45.030 --> 00:52:48.430
Therefore, we are all living
in a very small world.

00:52:48.430 --> 00:52:50.323
We are all global citizens.

00:52:51.890 --> 00:52:54.173
When I was 18 years old boy,

00:52:55.420 --> 00:52:58.580
I was very lucky boy at that time

00:52:59.680 --> 00:53:02.260
to have been invited to White House

00:53:02.260 --> 00:53:04.723
and met President Kennedy John F. Kennedy.

00:53:06.790 --> 00:53:11.790
I was not even able to meet
the mayor of my own city.

00:53:14.800 --> 00:53:19.527
Young boy, I was one
of 140 global students.

00:53:21.440 --> 00:53:23.333
What President Kennedy said,

00:53:25.107 --> 00:53:30.107
"The political leaders do
not engage each other."

00:53:30.420 --> 00:53:34.253
Of course, it was true during
the height of cold war.

00:53:35.330 --> 00:53:39.010
Khrushchev and Kennedy were
not talking each other.

00:53:39.010 --> 00:53:40.023
They were fighting.

00:53:41.350 --> 00:53:45.110
The national borders do not mean much.

00:53:45.110 --> 00:53:48.931
National borders meant much at that time.

00:53:48.931 --> 00:53:51.373
There was strict restrictions.

00:53:52.230 --> 00:53:54.780
So you could not go from
this country to another,

00:53:54.780 --> 00:53:57.583
without passport or whatever,

00:53:58.440 --> 00:54:00.010
particularly for Koreans

00:54:00.950 --> 00:54:05.950
where we were belonging to
this free democratic society

00:54:08.350 --> 00:54:12.090
were not allowed to visit
any communist country.

00:54:12.090 --> 00:54:17.003
But what he said, you young
people can do much better.

00:54:20.220 --> 00:54:23.990
The question was whether you are ready

00:54:23.990 --> 00:54:25.773
to extend your helping hand.

00:54:26.810 --> 00:54:30.120
That was inspiring, quite inspiring.

00:54:30.120 --> 00:54:32.130
I thought at that time,

00:54:32.130 --> 00:54:33.973
I was thinking, what should I do?

00:54:35.430 --> 00:54:39.010
If I say that I wanted to
become the Secretary-General,

00:54:39.010 --> 00:54:39.963
that's a lie.

00:54:41.400 --> 00:54:44.313
I thought, what should
I do for my country?

00:54:46.290 --> 00:54:49.370
Korea was very poor at that time,

00:54:49.370 --> 00:54:51.830
just a few years after Korean war.

00:54:51.830 --> 00:54:55.090
So I thought that I should
do something for my country,

00:54:55.090 --> 00:54:58.550
the reconstruction and
development of my country.

00:54:58.550 --> 00:55:00.580
That's why I became Secretary-General.

00:55:00.580 --> 00:55:05.580
And foreign minister of Korea later,

00:55:05.960 --> 00:55:10.200
when I became foreign minister of Korea,

00:55:10.200 --> 00:55:11.510
I was thinking,

00:55:11.510 --> 00:55:15.267
and my eyes were opening
wider than before.

00:55:17.940 --> 00:55:22.900
Then I was thinking, what
could I do for the world now?

00:55:22.900 --> 00:55:25.293
That's my confession.

00:55:26.320 --> 00:55:29.141
If I had such ambition from young

00:55:29.141 --> 00:55:32.533
to become Secretary-General
of the United Nations,

00:55:33.720 --> 00:55:36.184
but I think for you now,

00:55:36.184 --> 00:55:39.830
you may have some limited future dreams,

00:55:39.830 --> 00:55:42.340
but you can be practical,

00:55:42.340 --> 00:55:44.783
but you can have better visions.

00:55:45.650 --> 00:55:47.950
But what is important at this time,

00:55:47.950 --> 00:55:50.420
have some global vision,

00:55:50.420 --> 00:55:54.370
and global citizenship in your heart,

00:55:54.370 --> 00:55:57.120
that when you act as a global citizen,

00:55:57.120 --> 00:56:00.380
I'm sure that you'll make much, much more

00:56:00.380 --> 00:56:02.130
and better contribution

00:56:02.130 --> 00:56:05.140
to address all these global challenges.

00:56:05.140 --> 00:56:07.877
This is my message today to you

00:56:07.877 --> 00:56:11.252
and I hope that we all work together

00:56:11.252 --> 00:56:13.830
to make this world better.

00:56:13.830 --> 00:56:17.110
We cannot just leave this way

00:56:17.110 --> 00:56:19.093
to our succeeding generation.

00:56:20.070 --> 00:56:22.670
You may think that you
are not responsible,

00:56:22.670 --> 00:56:27.660
but at least you have a moral
responsibility as I am now.

00:56:27.660 --> 00:56:30.673
I don't have any political
responsibility now,

00:56:30.673 --> 00:56:33.042
but I have a moral responsibility.

00:56:33.042 --> 00:56:36.125
You also do have moral responsibility

00:56:37.350 --> 00:56:41.400
to work together in
solidarity, in partnership

00:56:43.220 --> 00:56:45.460
to make this world sustainable

00:56:45.460 --> 00:56:49.164
and more peaceful and prosperous.

00:56:49.164 --> 00:56:51.340
That's your moral responsibility.

00:56:51.340 --> 00:56:55.900
And let us try to make this world a better

00:56:55.900 --> 00:56:58.107
Thank very much for your attention.

00:56:58.107 --> 00:56:58.940
Thank you.

00:56:58.940 --> 00:57:01.940
(audience clapping)

00:57:10.170 --> 00:57:11.170
- [Participant] Everyone,
we'll take questions

00:57:11.170 --> 00:57:12.003
from the audience.

00:57:12.003 --> 00:57:12.836
If you could just raise your hand

00:57:12.836 --> 00:57:14.140
so we can bring you the mic.

00:57:21.940 --> 00:57:22.880
- Good evening.

00:57:25.520 --> 00:57:28.937
Mr. Ban, Thank you, first of
all, so much for this talk

00:57:28.937 --> 00:57:32.700
and for your efforts to
make our world better.

00:57:32.700 --> 00:57:34.360
My name is (indistinct).

00:57:34.360 --> 00:57:36.880
I am Fox International
Fellow here at Yale.

00:57:36.880 --> 00:57:39.860
And as you spoke about
the global citizenship,

00:57:39.860 --> 00:57:42.700
I consider myself as a global citizen,

00:57:42.700 --> 00:57:47.700
at least because this fellowship
considers us folks fellows

00:57:48.270 --> 00:57:50.460
as citizen ambassadors,

00:57:50.460 --> 00:57:55.460
and I study diplomacy and
international relations

00:57:56.320 --> 00:57:57.770
for 10 years.

00:57:57.770 --> 00:58:02.770
And I consider communication
as the essence of diplomacy.

00:58:02.800 --> 00:58:07.800
And as you spoke about
the race of our voice,

00:58:09.320 --> 00:58:14.133
I, as young woman who intends
to join the United Nations,

00:58:15.000 --> 00:58:20.000
I'm wondering how can I
make this voice heard?

00:58:21.870 --> 00:58:25.130
Because one thing is to raise voice.

00:58:25.130 --> 00:58:28.140
And the other thing is to be heard.

00:58:28.140 --> 00:58:28.973
Thank you.

00:58:30.700 --> 00:58:33.990
- There are many, many
ways for your children

00:58:33.990 --> 00:58:38.990
or yourself to contribute
to a current world.

00:58:39.080 --> 00:58:41.890
And I welcome anybody

00:58:41.890 --> 00:58:46.890
who really want to be working
for the United Nations.

00:58:48.050 --> 00:58:51.470
I think United Nations
is the most universal

00:58:52.320 --> 00:58:57.320
and global organizations for
any young talented people

00:58:58.150 --> 00:58:59.690
would be most welcome.

00:58:59.690 --> 00:59:04.240
But there are certain
procedures and regulations.

00:59:04.240 --> 00:59:09.240
So I hope you can just
make sure that all this,

00:59:12.440 --> 00:59:16.293
first of all, prepare for
all these examination,

00:59:17.650 --> 00:59:22.650
whatever test, then
opportunities are always open.

00:59:23.530 --> 00:59:28.530
But there are many students
while I was serving

00:59:29.880 --> 00:59:32.923
who really wanted to
start from internship.

00:59:34.590 --> 00:59:38.093
This is also competitive,
quite competitive.

00:59:39.540 --> 00:59:44.540
The UN selects at least 900,
1,200 interns every year,

00:59:49.890 --> 00:59:51.660
three-month internship.

00:59:51.660 --> 00:59:53.380
And there are many ways,

00:59:53.380 --> 00:59:58.200
and many organizations are
opening these positions.

00:59:58.200 --> 01:00:03.200
UN positions are always open to the world.

01:00:03.560 --> 01:00:06.960
It's not only limited to a UN staff

01:00:06.960 --> 01:00:08.520
whenever there is opening.

01:00:08.520 --> 01:00:13.520
Therefore I think your
friends or whoever maybe

01:00:14.450 --> 01:00:16.540
can have an opportunity,

01:00:16.540 --> 01:00:19.150
but you must be prepared for that.

01:00:19.150 --> 01:00:23.383
I hope that I have
addressed to your question.

01:00:29.470 --> 01:00:32.770
- [Mary] Thank you so
much, Secretary-General.

01:00:32.770 --> 01:00:34.270
My name's Mary Evelyn Tucker.

01:00:34.270 --> 01:00:35.920
I teach at the School of the Environment

01:00:35.920 --> 01:00:37.040
in the Divinity School.

01:00:37.040 --> 01:00:40.500
And I would be so grateful
if you would comment

01:00:40.500 --> 01:00:44.080
on the event that took
place at the United Nations,

01:00:44.080 --> 01:00:46.360
when the Pope came to speak

01:00:46.360 --> 01:00:51.360
and also his encyclical
Laudato si on the environment,

01:00:51.890 --> 01:00:55.630
which has also called people
to a global citizenship,

01:00:55.630 --> 01:00:57.450
but an intrical ecology

01:00:57.450 --> 01:01:00.630
that brings people and
the planet together.

01:01:00.630 --> 01:01:03.710
So I'd love to have your comment on that.

01:01:03.710 --> 01:01:05.510
And thank you so much for your talk.

01:01:07.380 --> 01:01:09.463
- So, is there question or comment?

01:01:11.010 --> 01:01:14.880
- [Mary] The question is, Laudato si

01:01:14.880 --> 01:01:17.350
is one of the most important documents

01:01:17.350 --> 01:01:22.350
in bringing a moral force
together for the environment.

01:01:22.550 --> 01:01:24.830
Many people feel it's unprecedented.

01:01:24.830 --> 01:01:28.600
And I wanted to have your
comment on the Pope's,

01:01:28.600 --> 01:01:30.700
both his speech at the United Nations,

01:01:30.700 --> 01:01:32.770
but also the encyclical,

01:01:32.770 --> 01:01:36.090
which he wrote to affect
the Paris Agreement

01:01:36.090 --> 01:01:38.143
in December of 2015.

01:01:39.050 --> 01:01:44.050
- I think as of now, Paris Climate Change

01:01:44.530 --> 01:01:47.570
sets very walkable...

01:01:50.060 --> 01:01:53.220
I don't think it is most ambitious,

01:01:53.220 --> 01:01:58.220
but it is a walkable path
to address climate change.

01:01:58.290 --> 01:02:02.570
And there are many, many ways

01:02:02.570 --> 01:02:05.340
we can further strengthen

01:02:06.566 --> 01:02:10.940
based on this Paris
Climate Change Agreement.

01:02:10.940 --> 01:02:15.350
Even reaching to this agreement

01:02:16.810 --> 01:02:21.727
had to make many
concessions and the some...

01:02:29.210 --> 01:02:31.450
So all negotiation, for example,

01:02:31.450 --> 01:02:34.403
all negotiations are not perfect one.

01:02:35.800 --> 01:02:38.913
I don't think the Paris
Agreement is a perfect one.

01:02:38.913 --> 01:02:42.150
Even with imperfect one,

01:02:42.150 --> 01:02:44.623
people are not implementing, as I said.

01:02:45.870 --> 01:02:50.870
If we do all what is said
in the Paris Agreement,

01:02:52.310 --> 01:02:54.600
I think we can at least

01:02:54.600 --> 01:02:59.600
prevent such a catastrophic consequences,

01:02:59.640 --> 01:03:02.270
but even in including United States

01:03:02.270 --> 01:03:05.797
are not keeping all commitment faithfully.

01:03:11.460 --> 01:03:16.460
For example, one thing really
disappointed me recently

01:03:18.970 --> 01:03:23.970
is only nature and absolutely
right and necessary.

01:03:24.810 --> 01:03:29.810
We provide humanitarian
assistance to people of Ukrainian.

01:03:30.610 --> 01:03:34.640
They deserve our support,
but that doesn't mean

01:03:34.640 --> 01:03:39.630
that this money for humanitarian support

01:03:39.630 --> 01:03:44.223
are slashed from climate
fundings, climate funds.

01:03:45.450 --> 01:03:50.450
US has promised 11 at
least $11 billion a year

01:03:52.040 --> 01:03:55.730
for climate out of a
hundred billion dollars.

01:03:55.730 --> 01:03:59.760
Then President Biden
just to cut $10 billion

01:03:59.760 --> 01:04:03.760
out of 11 and sent to Ukraine first.

01:04:03.760 --> 01:04:07.763
Of course, saving lives
may be much more urgent,

01:04:08.720 --> 01:04:11.713
but saving our planet is also urgent.

01:04:14.100 --> 01:04:19.100
Many European countries are
now slashing such fundings

01:04:19.380 --> 01:04:22.993
from climate fundings.

01:04:23.990 --> 01:04:27.050
That is what I have been
speaking out and criticizing

01:04:28.510 --> 01:04:31.940
through my oped or my speeches.

01:04:31.940 --> 01:04:33.563
This is shortsightedness.

01:04:35.204 --> 01:04:38.617
So don't blame that Paris climate changing

01:04:39.820 --> 01:04:43.010
may not be a perfect one.

01:04:43.010 --> 01:04:46.780
There is no such a perfect
constitution, perfect laws.

01:04:46.780 --> 01:04:51.720
This is all out of a negotiation.

01:04:51.720 --> 01:04:55.053
We could have been much,
much more ambitious,

01:04:56.020 --> 01:05:01.020
much, much tougher, but that
was all I could have done.

01:05:03.020 --> 01:05:07.270
Do you know how many years has it taken

01:05:07.270 --> 01:05:11.233
from since 1992 Kyoto Protocol 2015,

01:05:12.700 --> 01:05:17.023
it almost took 30 years.

01:05:17.910 --> 01:05:20.880
1992 Kyoto Protocol

01:05:20.880 --> 01:05:25.880
was the result out of almost
a 10 years negotiations.

01:05:27.050 --> 01:05:30.570
Then Kyoto Protocol became almost useless

01:05:31.870 --> 01:05:35.163
because US did not join.

01:05:36.250 --> 01:05:39.840
US signed it but was not ratified

01:05:39.840 --> 01:05:41.703
by the Parliament, Senate.

01:05:43.690 --> 01:05:48.690
So Al Gore, as a vice
president, he proudly signed it,

01:05:48.900 --> 01:05:51.410
but it became useless
for the United States

01:05:53.120 --> 01:05:56.930
and then means when US didn't join,

01:05:56.930 --> 01:06:00.690
then Kyoto Protocol become almost dead

01:06:00.690 --> 01:06:03.337
from the moment it became effective.

01:06:05.970 --> 01:06:10.330
So there was a lot of such
frustrating processes,

01:06:10.330 --> 01:06:13.820
and that's why as soon as
I became Secretary-General,

01:06:13.820 --> 01:06:17.460
I made it number one top priority.

01:06:17.460 --> 01:06:22.460
And I first tried to convince
President Bush at that time.

01:06:24.830 --> 01:06:29.703
He was not convinced, but at
the end, he was convinced.

01:06:31.430 --> 01:06:32.600
He was convinced.

01:06:32.600 --> 01:06:35.040
And I was very much grateful to him

01:06:35.040 --> 01:06:38.443
because he just added his voice.

01:06:39.680 --> 01:06:44.400
Reluctantly then made
the beginning of success.

01:06:46.580 --> 01:06:51.580
Since he said yes, then it
took nine years from 2007,

01:06:53.850 --> 01:06:57.503
then 2000 until 2015.

01:06:58.550 --> 01:07:02.760
As I said, I don't know, I
felt how much I was lucky

01:07:03.960 --> 01:07:08.293
because that was adopted
before Trump came in.

01:07:10.210 --> 01:07:15.210
If Trump joined this
presidency a little earlier,

01:07:16.190 --> 01:07:19.100
I don't think we have, even now,

01:07:19.100 --> 01:07:23.050
we don't have this
climate agreement, yeah.

01:07:23.050 --> 01:07:25.920
Again, there is no such
a perfect one, yeah.

01:07:25.920 --> 01:07:27.530
- [Participant] That's
a follow-up question

01:07:28.558 --> 01:07:29.740
to the previous question.

01:07:29.740 --> 01:07:33.907
So Catholic church has
more than 1 billion members

01:07:35.090 --> 01:07:37.183
around the world, and-

01:07:37.183 --> 01:07:38.830
- 1 Billion, 1 billion.

01:07:38.830 --> 01:07:39.663
- [Participant] 1 Billion.

01:07:39.663 --> 01:07:44.350
So do you think there's any
role the Catholic Pope can play

01:07:44.350 --> 01:07:47.873
to really enforce this Paris
Environmental Agreement?

01:07:49.360 --> 01:07:54.360
- The religious leaders have
a very important role to play,

01:07:54.920 --> 01:07:59.920
and I am deeply grateful to Pope Francis

01:08:01.440 --> 01:08:06.440
in making sure that 1
billion Catholic believers,

01:08:10.100 --> 01:08:12.230
I mean, churchgoers.

01:08:12.230 --> 01:08:14.035
He instructed,

01:08:14.035 --> 01:08:17.792
and I discuss this matter
with the Pope himself

01:08:17.792 --> 01:08:22.792
at the United Nations, as
well as in Rome Vatican,

01:08:23.750 --> 01:08:26.220
I visit Vatican two, three times,

01:08:26.220 --> 01:08:31.220
to make sure that Catholic
community would join.

01:08:32.440 --> 01:08:37.440
And he issued his decree
to all 1 billion Catholics.

01:08:41.410 --> 01:08:43.630
That really helped a lot.

01:08:43.630 --> 01:08:45.170
That really helped a lot.

01:08:45.170 --> 01:08:48.943
I forgot the name of this
decree, but in any way,

01:08:51.720 --> 01:08:56.720
after that, I went to
Vatican after my retirement.

01:08:57.070 --> 01:09:01.240
I think ambassador Kim, he was with me

01:09:01.240 --> 01:09:05.270
to pay my great thanks to him.

01:09:05.270 --> 01:09:09.120
Then another contribution he made

01:09:10.190 --> 01:09:14.580
was he came to the United Nations twice,

01:09:14.580 --> 01:09:18.350
according to all history
of the United Nations,

01:09:18.350 --> 01:09:21.420
all the popes, previous popes,

01:09:21.420 --> 01:09:26.420
they came just once in
their pope leadership,

01:09:28.140 --> 01:09:30.230
once to the United Nations.

01:09:30.230 --> 01:09:35.230
Then current Pope Francis
is the only exception.

01:09:35.450 --> 01:09:40.170
He came twice to urge member state

01:09:40.170 --> 01:09:42.460
to agree on associated with development

01:09:42.460 --> 01:09:46.820
and to agree on climate change.

01:09:46.820 --> 01:09:51.820
He gave such a full
blessings, full blessings.

01:09:52.130 --> 01:09:53.960
Of course, he's not a politician,

01:09:53.960 --> 01:09:58.010
so he could not exert
the political pressure,

01:09:58.010 --> 01:10:02.343
but he has given his full blessings.

01:10:03.830 --> 01:10:08.060
I think I'm still very much
grateful for his intervention.

01:10:08.060 --> 01:10:09.047
Yeah, thank you.

01:10:12.900 --> 01:10:13.733
Yes.

01:10:21.550 --> 01:10:22.910
- Hello, Mr. Ban.

01:10:22.910 --> 01:10:24.200
Thank you so much for coming here.

01:10:24.200 --> 01:10:27.390
My name is Eli, and I'm
a second-year MBA student

01:10:27.390 --> 01:10:28.973
of Yale School of Management.

01:10:30.180 --> 01:10:32.880
First thing, I do feel much better

01:10:32.880 --> 01:10:35.860
as a private citizen
after hearing your speech.

01:10:35.860 --> 01:10:38.480
At least I can say something

01:10:38.480 --> 01:10:40.640
if I feel something is wrong.

01:10:40.640 --> 01:10:43.444
and I feel your pain
as a Secretary-General

01:10:43.444 --> 01:10:47.820
of the United Nations, and
you need to try your best

01:10:47.820 --> 01:10:51.263
to keep everybody stand together.

01:10:52.230 --> 01:10:53.820
Second, I do have a question

01:10:53.820 --> 01:10:55.700
for your forward-looking plan

01:10:55.700 --> 01:10:59.150
as the President of the
Ban Ki-Moon Foundation.

01:10:59.150 --> 01:11:00.040
Do you have any plans

01:11:00.040 --> 01:11:04.410
to continue the Carbon
Neutralization Initiative

01:11:04.410 --> 01:11:05.430
for the next step?

01:11:07.650 --> 01:11:11.953
- So, what do you want about
Ban Ki-Moon Foundation?

01:11:12.900 --> 01:11:14.683
- So, for the next step,

01:11:15.520 --> 01:11:18.620
do you have any plans, or do
you want to share any thoughts

01:11:18.620 --> 01:11:21.523
how do you want to utilize this platform?

01:11:24.350 --> 01:11:27.815
- I should confess that
this Ban Ki-Moon Foundation

01:11:27.815 --> 01:11:30.673
was established just three years ago,

01:11:32.442 --> 01:11:35.270
with some humble commitment

01:11:37.840 --> 01:11:42.180
that I should do whatever I can

01:11:42.180 --> 01:11:46.563
to make our future better for everyone.

01:11:47.838 --> 01:11:50.090
This is a small foundation

01:11:50.090 --> 01:11:54.490
composed of some small group of people,

01:11:54.490 --> 01:11:58.710
but I have a limited
capacity in doing that.

01:11:58.710 --> 01:12:03.710
I am using my role as a
former Secretary-General,

01:12:04.860 --> 01:12:07.424
my moral voice,

01:12:07.424 --> 01:12:12.424
working in close
cooperation and association

01:12:12.710 --> 01:12:16.973
with the other organizations
and governments.

01:12:19.420 --> 01:12:22.900
When I was introduced by professor Kim,

01:12:22.900 --> 01:12:27.140
I'm now holding many
and different positions.

01:12:27.140 --> 01:12:30.323
For example, when it comes to climate,

01:12:31.660 --> 01:12:36.660
I am Chairman of Global
Center on Adaptation.

01:12:42.880 --> 01:12:47.880
There are two policy responses to climate.

01:12:48.080 --> 01:12:51.943
One is mitigation and adaptation.

01:12:51.943 --> 01:12:54.970
Mitigation, we know that
we have to mitigate.

01:12:54.970 --> 01:12:59.123
We have to reduce carbon emissions,

01:13:00.060 --> 01:13:04.740
but how to invest wisely for the future

01:13:04.740 --> 01:13:09.740
to prevent any future
disruptive climate consequences,

01:13:12.020 --> 01:13:13.653
that is adaptation.

01:13:14.640 --> 01:13:19.640
For example, when you build
the buildings, infrastructures,

01:13:20.180 --> 01:13:24.513
when you build the street, I mean roads,

01:13:25.630 --> 01:13:30.630
or when you plant to some crops,

01:13:30.720 --> 01:13:35.720
then how can you solve these
climate-resilient crops?

01:13:38.730 --> 01:13:42.619
Or how can you build
climate-resilient buildings

01:13:42.619 --> 01:13:44.473
and infrastructures?

01:13:45.530 --> 01:13:50.190
There are many ways we can save money

01:13:50.190 --> 01:13:55.050
from future possible
destruction caused by climate.

01:13:55.050 --> 01:14:00.050
Those are the much more,
I mean, better ideas.

01:14:01.210 --> 01:14:04.230
I'm working as a chairman of this one.

01:14:04.230 --> 01:14:07.510
And as I said, I'm
working also as a chairman

01:14:07.510 --> 01:14:10.600
of this Global Green Growth Institute.

01:14:10.600 --> 01:14:14.490
And I'm also working in other...

01:14:14.490 --> 01:14:18.730
I'm using all different organization.

01:14:18.730 --> 01:14:20.610
I'm also working as a chairman

01:14:20.610 --> 01:14:24.440
of this Ban Ki-Moon Center
for Global Citizens.

01:14:24.440 --> 01:14:29.440
Global citizenship, we don't
have any tools or resources

01:14:30.520 --> 01:14:33.470
like you build a house or whatever,

01:14:33.470 --> 01:14:38.470
but we teach them what needs to be done

01:14:41.260 --> 01:14:45.040
to prevent further disruptive consequences

01:14:45.040 --> 01:14:46.627
caused by climate change.

01:14:46.627 --> 01:14:51.627
And we also just try to urge
world leaders to empower women,

01:14:53.550 --> 01:14:58.550
empower youth, protect the human rights

01:14:58.580 --> 01:15:01.240
and do much more on climate issues

01:15:01.240 --> 01:15:05.883
and make a sustainable
development, etcetera, et cetera.

01:15:06.820 --> 01:15:11.820
I'm using my voice as a
former Secretary-General.

01:15:12.730 --> 01:15:15.490
These are something which I am doing.

01:15:15.490 --> 01:15:18.050
So if you add your voices,

01:15:18.050 --> 01:15:20.630
and I have the much, much...

01:15:20.630 --> 01:15:25.630
I have a stronger voice,
stronger microphones, so to say.

01:15:27.000 --> 01:15:27.890
Thank you very much.

01:15:27.890 --> 01:15:28.723
Thank you, yeah.

01:15:31.785 --> 01:15:33.580
- So we're gonna have one more question

01:15:33.580 --> 01:15:37.470
and then we prepared the
reception on the second floor.

01:15:37.470 --> 01:15:40.110
So all of you are welcome to join

01:15:40.110 --> 01:15:42.840
and keep asking
Secretary-General more questions.

01:15:42.840 --> 01:15:43.970
Okay, one more question.

01:15:43.970 --> 01:15:46.260
Hwansoo, do you wanna
pick the last question?

01:15:46.260 --> 01:15:47.093
- Somebody?

01:15:50.631 --> 01:15:54.050
(audience laughing)

01:15:54.050 --> 01:15:55.950
- The most difficult decision to make.

01:15:57.770 --> 01:15:59.050
Okay, you and you,

01:15:59.050 --> 01:16:01.010
maybe you can explain your two questions

01:16:01.010 --> 01:16:03.813
and maybe Secretary-General
can answer together.

01:16:06.954 --> 01:16:09.871
(speaking faintly)

01:16:19.680 --> 01:16:21.580
- Thank you, Secretary-General,
for your speech.

01:16:21.580 --> 01:16:25.153
I'm Chris, an undergraduate
student from South Korea.

01:16:25.153 --> 01:16:27.030
I think I speak for everyone in this room

01:16:27.030 --> 01:16:30.160
when I say you are a
symbol of collaboration

01:16:30.160 --> 01:16:33.730
for worldwide collaboration
and global collaboration.

01:16:33.730 --> 01:16:38.240
The COVID 19 pandemic has
changed our individual lives.

01:16:38.240 --> 01:16:39.650
But in addition,

01:16:39.650 --> 01:16:41.890
we've seen that it requires
us to work together,

01:16:41.890 --> 01:16:44.540
to wear masks, to get vaccinated,

01:16:44.540 --> 01:16:48.010
to be aware of spreading
the disease to others.

01:16:48.010 --> 01:16:50.640
I see all of these as
acts of collaboration.

01:16:50.640 --> 01:16:52.680
Have you seen any instances

01:16:52.680 --> 01:16:55.020
where the pandemic has
changed collaboration

01:16:55.020 --> 01:16:56.490
on the global level

01:16:56.490 --> 01:17:00.683
and has it affected
collaboration between countries?

01:17:06.317 --> 01:17:07.380
- So, you are now talking

01:17:07.380 --> 01:17:11.160
about all these vaccine issues now.

01:17:11.160 --> 01:17:12.280
- [Chris] How it has changed

01:17:12.280 --> 01:17:14.813
the way we negotiate issues globally.

01:17:17.830 --> 01:17:21.310
- I have not been engaged
in this negotiation

01:17:21.310 --> 01:17:23.850
since I'm out of United Nations,

01:17:23.850 --> 01:17:25.893
but during my time,

01:17:27.270 --> 01:17:32.270
whenever such kind of global
health problems happened,

01:17:32.740 --> 01:17:36.153
of course, I had to work
very close with WHO.

01:17:37.393 --> 01:17:41.460
WHO is the main organization

01:17:41.460 --> 01:17:45.130
which deals with all these
global health issues.

01:17:45.130 --> 01:17:48.730
But WHO, frankly speaking,

01:17:48.730 --> 01:17:51.500
is a very small organization

01:17:51.500 --> 01:17:55.910
among 16 specialized agencies.

01:17:55.910 --> 01:17:57.820
They're a bigger organization,

01:17:57.820 --> 01:18:00.980
but they are mostly research organization

01:18:00.980 --> 01:18:04.960
and doesn't have operational capacities.

01:18:04.960 --> 01:18:08.730
So that's why from the beginning,

01:18:08.730 --> 01:18:13.730
there was some problems in
addressing pandemic issues.

01:18:14.980 --> 01:18:18.690
I immediately thought that the WHO,

01:18:18.690 --> 01:18:22.677
you may know president Trump
has been criticizing WHO,

01:18:25.074 --> 01:18:30.074
then maybe half right, half wrong.

01:18:31.120 --> 01:18:36.120
But why I'm critical is that
president Trump was wrong

01:18:38.490 --> 01:18:43.087
in withdrawing instead
of supporting the WHO.

01:18:44.608 --> 01:18:49.608
Whatever mistakes or some visa
handling at the beginning,

01:18:53.350 --> 01:18:54.283
there may be.

01:18:55.390 --> 01:18:58.930
US should have given much bigger

01:18:58.930 --> 01:19:00.883
and stronger support for WHO

01:19:02.290 --> 01:19:06.220
rather than just withdrawing
and cutting funding.

01:19:06.220 --> 01:19:09.823
Then WHO couldn't have any role to play.

01:19:11.380 --> 01:19:16.160
Then I telephone as a
former Secretary-General,

01:19:16.160 --> 01:19:20.210
I telephone Dr. Tedros,
WHO Director-General,

01:19:20.210 --> 01:19:25.210
look, this is going to
be a very serious issue.

01:19:27.430 --> 01:19:32.430
I telephone March 2020 or March of 2020.

01:19:33.620 --> 01:19:36.860
You better walk and consult

01:19:36.860 --> 01:19:38.691
with the Secretary-General
of the United Nations

01:19:38.691 --> 01:19:43.691
and mobilize a whole United
nations organization.

01:19:44.010 --> 01:19:46.123
Only Secretary-General can mobilize all,

01:19:47.260 --> 01:19:48.893
not the Director-General of WHO.

01:19:50.110 --> 01:19:53.840
If US intervened from the beginning,

01:19:53.840 --> 01:19:58.570
I think US could mobilize
such a global run.

01:19:58.570 --> 01:20:00.180
That's a US President

01:20:00.180 --> 01:20:02.510
and Secretary-General
of the United Nations,

01:20:02.510 --> 01:20:04.370
I think there are two

01:20:04.370 --> 01:20:09.370
who can mobilize global
resources, but he didn't,

01:20:11.040 --> 01:20:15.313
and President Trump withdrew,
just cutoff fundings.

01:20:16.240 --> 01:20:20.563
That's the beginning of
worst things happening now.

01:20:22.060 --> 01:20:24.610
I already learned from the beginning.

01:20:24.610 --> 01:20:26.070
Now, looking back,

01:20:26.070 --> 01:20:30.353
I think we must learn all
these lessons from failures.

01:20:33.220 --> 01:20:35.200
We failed, in fact,

01:20:35.200 --> 01:20:39.310
we could have prevented
it much, much quicker,

01:20:39.310 --> 01:20:41.360
much more effective way.

01:20:41.360 --> 01:20:46.360
And I think I said that
this is an issue of justice.

01:20:50.940 --> 01:20:55.450
Just vaccinating American
people didn't mean much

01:20:55.450 --> 01:21:00.450
because viruses do not
care about the borders.

01:21:00.700 --> 01:21:04.870
People have been traveling
at the beginning,

01:21:04.870 --> 01:21:08.100
at the beginning, and
therefore we had learning.

01:21:08.100 --> 01:21:11.810
If you do not learn from the past failure,

01:21:11.810 --> 01:21:14.393
you are apt to repeat the same things.

01:21:15.320 --> 01:21:20.320
In 2014, when Ebola
broke out in West Africa,

01:21:22.940 --> 01:21:27.673
the United Nations just took
one day to make a decision,

01:21:28.820 --> 01:21:33.820
while coronavirus case,
it took four months.

01:21:37.072 --> 01:21:40.763
There was a big difference
from one day to four-month.

01:21:41.910 --> 01:21:44.790
The Security Council, I reported
to the Security Council.

01:21:44.790 --> 01:21:47.360
Look, this Ebola,

01:21:47.360 --> 01:21:50.750
which is now shipping
through Western Africa,

01:21:50.750 --> 01:21:53.420
is not normal one.

01:21:53.420 --> 01:21:57.463
We must take urgent action,
mobilize all the resources.

01:22:00.610 --> 01:22:01.903
The Security Council,

01:22:03.270 --> 01:22:05.570
there was the reason why Security Council

01:22:05.570 --> 01:22:08.310
is involved in these health issues

01:22:08.310 --> 01:22:13.310
because health can create
lot of global security issues

01:22:14.580 --> 01:22:17.383
like COVID 19 has been doing.

01:22:18.779 --> 01:22:23.480
The Security Council adopted
urgent resolution by one day,

01:22:23.480 --> 01:22:28.000
that Ebola is a serious threat

01:22:28.000 --> 01:22:30.003
to international peace and security.

01:22:31.920 --> 01:22:33.970
Of course, they mentioned health issues,

01:22:33.970 --> 01:22:37.483
but is a issue of peace and security.

01:22:38.770 --> 01:22:42.433
We organized Ebola mission.

01:22:44.520 --> 01:22:49.520
United States sent 7,000
soldiers, military soldiers,

01:22:51.710 --> 01:22:56.710
French 500, United Kingdom 750 soldiers.

01:22:57.030 --> 01:23:01.070
They were all dispatched
immediately to Western Africa.

01:23:01.070 --> 01:23:04.083
They barricaded all borders.

01:23:05.200 --> 01:23:08.980
Then there were not much traffic
among these infected areas.

01:23:08.980 --> 01:23:13.980
And I myself risked my
life to visit the areas.

01:23:14.350 --> 01:23:17.663
I was quarantined the
21 days after returning,

01:23:19.450 --> 01:23:23.223
but United Nations and World
Bank didn't do anything,

01:23:24.310 --> 01:23:25.243
not much.

01:23:27.690 --> 01:23:31.770
This is the main reason
because Security Council,

01:23:31.770 --> 01:23:35.660
because of the conflict between
United States and China,

01:23:35.660 --> 01:23:38.463
China boycotted.

01:23:39.670 --> 01:23:44.670
It took four months to
declare that COVID 19

01:23:46.750 --> 01:23:50.890
is a serious threat to
international peace and security.

01:23:50.890 --> 01:23:52.360
After four months,

01:23:52.360 --> 01:23:54.770
most of the countries have been devastated

01:23:55.900 --> 01:23:57.510
just to get in four months.

01:23:57.510 --> 01:24:02.510
If there was an urgent
action blocking somewhere,

01:24:03.240 --> 01:24:08.140
and if countries really
collaborated each other.

01:24:08.140 --> 01:24:13.140
So this kind of China American conflict

01:24:13.160 --> 01:24:17.390
even affected this global health issues.

01:24:17.390 --> 01:24:19.160
So this is quite regrettable.

01:24:19.160 --> 01:24:24.050
So we have to learn
all these hard lessons,

01:24:24.050 --> 01:24:25.650
not to repeat.

01:24:25.650 --> 01:24:27.810
Not to repeat in the future.

01:24:27.810 --> 01:24:29.620
- Yeah, I hope the global leader

01:24:29.620 --> 01:24:34.620
learned from this two-year-long
pandemic-like period.

01:24:35.550 --> 01:24:39.570
Anyway, maybe I would give you the honor

01:24:39.570 --> 01:24:42.980
to ask him in person on the second floor.

01:24:42.980 --> 01:24:44.560
How did it sound?

01:24:44.560 --> 01:24:46.270
Okay, thank you so much.

01:24:46.270 --> 01:24:48.096
Thank you, Secretary-General,
for your wonderful talk.

01:24:48.096 --> 01:24:48.929
(audience clapping)

01:24:48.929 --> 01:24:50.658
Please give him a round of applause.

01:24:50.658 --> 01:24:51.991
- So, thank you.

01:24:55.296 --> 01:24:57.879
(upbeat music)

